    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All 42 tests pass with `BUILD SUCCESS`, including the new `Cp29Tests` (3/3).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the optional, city-region-validated `postcode` field for owners on `POST /api/owners`, keeping the request contract backward-compatible (postcode validated only when present).\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added the `postcode` property to the `OwnerFields` schema (`type: string`, `pattern: ^[0-9]{4}$`). Since `Owner` is `allOf` `OwnerFields`, this generates the field on both the request DTO (`OwnerFieldsDto`) and the response DTO (`OwnerDto`). The bean-validation pattern gives an automatic 400 for a malformed (non-4-digit) postcode.\n\n2. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added the `postcode` column plus getter/setter.\n\n3. **DB schemas** (hsqldb, h2, postgres, mysql `schema.sql`) \u2014 Added the `postcode` column to the `owners` table. The test suite runs on the hsqldb schema.\n\n4. **`rest/advice/InvalidPostcodeException.java`** (new) + **`ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`** \u2014 New exception and a handler translating it to a 400 with `errors: [\"postcode\"]`, mirroring the existing `InvalidEmailException` pattern.\n\n5. **`rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 Added the fixed city\u2192region table (Sydney\u2192NSW, Melbourne\u2192VIC, Brisbane\u2192QLD) and region\u2192range table (NSW 2000-2099, VIC 3000-3099, QLD 4000-4099), plus a `validatePostcode` method wired into `addOwner`. It skips `null` (optional), enforces 4 digits, and \u2014 only when the city has a known region \u2014 enforces the range. A city with no known region accepts any 4-digit postcode. The value is persisted and returned via the existing MapStruct mapping (matched by name, so no mapper edits were needed).",
